# East Asia batch (1894-2019): every brief rewritten to rules zero to four.
OVERRIDE = True
A = {
"as189440": dict(
 bluf="Japan fought Qing China in 1894 to end Chinese control of Korea and take China's place as the leading power in East Asia. Its modernised army and navy won every major engagement in nine months, and the Treaty of Shimonoseki gave Japan Taiwan, an indemnity of 200 million taels and a free hand in Korea. Leadership of East Asia passed from China to Japan, and the exposed weakness of the Qing set off a European scramble for concessions in China.",
 now="The war moved leadership in East Asia from China to Japan, gave Japan Taiwan and a free hand in Korea, and exposed the Qing to the scramble for concessions that followed; it began fifty years of Japanese expansion that ended in 1945.",
 story="Korea was a Qing tributary in which Japan had built commercial and political influence since 1876. The Donghak peasant rebellion of 1894 brought Chinese troops at the Korean court's request and Japanese troops in answer. Japanese Prime Minister Itō Hirobumi's government declared war on 1 August 1894. Japan's army beat Qing forces at Pyongyang in September, its navy won at the Yalu the same month, and its troops took Port Arthur in November 1894 and Weihaiwei in February 1895. Qing viceroy Li Hongzhang signed peace at Shimonoseki in April 1895.",
 why="Rivalry over Korea came first: China treated it as a tributary and Japan's planners saw it as a dagger pointed at Japan's heart. Japan's Meiji reforms had produced a conscript army and a modern fleet while Qing self-strengthening had produced ships without a unified command.",
 trig="The Donghak peasant rebellion in Korea, which led both China and Japan to send troops in 1894.",
 then="China ceded Taiwan and the Pescadores and paid 200 million taels. Russia, France and Germany forced Japan to return the Liaodong Peninsula within a week and took Chinese leases for themselves from 1897. Chinese reformers concluded that the Qing system had failed; the Hundred Days' Reform of 1898 and the revolution of 1911 followed.",
 fact="It was the first time an Asian state beat China with Western methods. The Qing fleet lost five ships at the Yalu and Japan lost none."),
"as189541": dict(
 bluf="Japan demanded Taiwan at Shimonoseki to gain a southern base and a colony that would mark it as a great power. Qing officials on the island declared a Republic of Formosa in May 1895; Japanese troops entered Taipei in June and took the last resisting city in October, and fifty years of colonial rule followed. Taiwan's development ran on a separate track from mainland China's, which is one root of the identity question that divides the strait today.",
 now="Fifty years of Japanese rule from 1895 to 1945 gave Taiwan railways, schools and a legal order separate from the mainland's. That separate experience, followed by Nationalist rule from 1945, is one root of a Taiwanese identity that Beijing reads as a provincial variation of Chinese identity and that many in Taiwan read as national.",
 story="Under the Treaty of Shimonoseki of April 1895 Qing China ceded Taiwan and the Pescadores to Japan. Qing governor Tang Jingsong and local gentry declared a Republic of Formosa in May 1895 to resist the transfer. Japanese troops landed in late May, entered Taipei in June and took Tainan in October; the republic was gone within months. Armed resistance continued for years, and from 1898 Japanese Governor-General Kodama Gentarō and civil administrator Gotō Shinpei ran the colony.",
 why="Japan wanted a southern base facing Fujian and the Philippines, a colony to prove great-power rank, and the island's sugar and camphor.",
 then="Japan crushed armed resistance over seven years at a cost of thousands of Taiwanese lives, then built railways, ports, schools and sugar mills to make Taiwan a model colony. From 1937 it pressed Japanese names and language on the population. In 1945 the island passed to Chiang Kai-shek's Republic of China.",
 fact="The Republic of Formosa lasted about five months and is often called Asia's first republic."),
"jp-queenmin": dict(
 bluf="Japanese minister in Seoul Miura Gorō organised the murder of Korean Queen Min to remove the court's leading advocate of leaning on Russia against Japan. Japanese agents and Korean allies killed her in Gyeongbok Palace on 8 October 1895 and burned her body. The killing drove King Gojong into the Russian legation instead of securing Japan's hold, and the Russo-Japanese rivalry over Korea sharpened toward the war of 1904.",
 now="The murder pushed Korea toward Russia from 1896 and turned the contest for Korea into the direct Russo-Japanese rivalry that ended in war in 1904. In Korea-Japan relations it stands first in the list of grievances from the colonial period.",
 story="After Russia, France and Germany forced Japan to return the Liaodong Peninsula in April 1895, Korean Queen Min, consort of King Gojong, turned to Russia to balance Japanese power. Japanese minister Miura Gorō organised an attack on Gyeongbok Palace in the early hours of 8 October 1895. Japanese agents and Korean soldiers trained by Japan broke in, killed the queen and burned her body.",
 why="Miura feared Korea would slide into Russia's sphere after the Triple Intervention. Queen Min and her clan were the pro-Russian and anti-Japanese centre of the court.",
 then="King Gojong fled to the Russian legation in February 1896 and ruled from it for a year; Russian advisers and concessions followed, and the rivalry between Japan and Russia sharpened. In 1897 Gojong proclaimed the Korean Empire and buried Min as Empress Myeongseong.",
 fact="A Japanese court in Hiroshima released Miura and his accomplices in January 1896 for want of evidence."),
"jp-shimonoseki": dict(
 bluf="Japanese Prime Minister Itō Hirobumi set out to turn victory over China into territory, money and a free hand in Korea. Qing viceroy Li Hongzhang conceded Taiwan, the Pescadores, the Liaodong Peninsula, 200 million taels and Korea's 'independence'. Japan became an imperial power with a treasury to fund a navy and the gold standard, and the Qing court's exposed weakness opened the reform crisis of 1898 and the foreign scramble for Chinese ports.",
 now="The indemnity funded Japan's naval expansion after 1896 and its move to the gold standard in 1897, which gave it access to London capital for the war against Russia in 1904.",
 story="Talks opened at Shimonoseki in March 1895 between Japanese Prime Minister Itō Hirobumi and Qing viceroy Li Hongzhang. On 24 March a Japanese nationalist shot Li in the face; he survived, and Japan softened its terms. The treaty signed on 17 April 1895 recognised Korea's 'independence', ending China's influence there; ceded Taiwan, the Pescadores and the Liaodong Peninsula; opened ports; and set an indemnity of 200 million taels of silver.",
 why="China had been beaten on land and at sea and feared a Japanese march on Beijing. Japan wanted its gains fixed before the European powers intervened.",
 then="Within six days Russia, France and Germany demanded that Japan give back Liaodong. Japan complied for a further 30 million taels and spent the combined indemnity on its navy.",
 fact="Li Hongzhang finished the talks with the assassin's bullet still lodged below his left eye."),
"jp-triple": dict(
 bluf="Russia, with France and Germany, set out to stop Japan holding the Liaodong Peninsula, which Russia wanted for its own warm-water port. Japan, unable to fight three European powers, returned the peninsula for 30 million taels. Tokyo drew the lesson that only armed strength would bring respect, and ten years of naval building aimed at Russia followed.",
 now="The intervention turned Japanese policy toward armament against Russia from 1895 to 1904 and is the standard case of a humiliation setting a state's foreign policy; Russia's seizure of the same port in 1898 made the war of 1904 likelier.",
 story="On 23 April 1895, six days after the Treaty of Shimonoseki, the ministers of Russia, France and Germany in Tokyo 'advised' Japan to give up the Liaodong Peninsula, including Port Arthur. Britain refused to join them. Japanese Prime Minister Itō Hirobumi's cabinet, facing three great powers with a fleet worn by war, gave the peninsula back for an extra 30 million taels.",
 why="Russian Tsar Nicholas II's government wanted a warm-water port in Manchuria. France was Russia's ally under the treaty of 1894. German Kaiser Wilhelm II wanted Russia's attention on Asia and away from Germany's eastern border.",
 then="Japan adopted the slogan 'gashin shōtan', enduring hardship for revenge, and built up its navy in British yards. Russia leased Port Arthur in 1898, Britain allied with Japan in 1902 and Japan attacked Russia in 1904.",
 myth="The intervening powers are often given as Britain and France, or the dispute placed in Korea. The powers were Russia, France and Germany, the territory was the Liaodong Peninsula, and Britain stayed out and became Japan's ally.",
 fact="'Gashin shōtan' comes from a Chinese tale of a king who slept on firewood and tasted gall each day to keep his hunger for revenge fresh."),
"jp-portarthur": dict(
 bluf="Russian Tsar Nicholas II's government forced Qing China to lease it the tip of the Liaodong Peninsula, the ground it had made Japan return three years earlier, to gain an ice-free naval base on the Pacific. It got Port Arthur and Dalian for 25 years and a railway linking them to the Trans-Siberian line. Japanese resentment became direct strategic rivalry, and war between the two within a decade became likelier.",
 now="The lease put a Russian fleet and railway in southern Manchuria from 1898 and turned the insult of 1895 into a standing threat; it set the ground and the object of the Russo-Japanese War of 1904-05.",
 story="In March 1898 Russian Tsar Nicholas II's government forced the Qing court to lease it the tip of the Liaodong Peninsula, with Port Arthur (Lüshun) and Dalian, for 25 years, and began a railway linking the ports to its Trans-Siberian line across Manchuria. It was the territory Russia, France and Germany had made Japan hand back three years earlier. Germany had just taken Qingdao, and Britain answered by taking Weihaiwei.",
 why="Russia wanted a warm-water Pacific port, since Vladivostok froze in winter, and was extending its railway across Manchuria.",
 then="Russian troops occupied Manchuria during the Boxer Rebellion in 1900 and stayed. Japan formed an alliance with Britain in 1902 and, after talks on spheres of influence failed, attacked Port Arthur in February 1904.",
 fact="Russia held Port Arthur for less than seven years; its garrison surrendered to Japan in January 1905."),
"as190043": dict(
 bluf="The Boxers, a militia movement in north China, set out to drive foreigners and Christians from the country, and Qing Empress Dowager Cixi backed them with a declaration of war on the powers in June 1900. An eight-nation army relieved the besieged legations in Beijing in August, looted the capital and imposed an indemnity of 450 million taels. The dynasty's authority was discredited at home and abroad, and the revolution that ended it came within eleven years.",
 now="The Boxer Protocol of 1901 put foreign troops in Beijing and a 39-year indemnity on Chinese revenue, which discredited the Qing and fed the nationalism behind the revolution of 1911. The episode is a fixed part of the 'century of humiliation' narrative the Communist Party uses to argue that only a strong state keeps foreign powers out.",
 story="The Boxers, a militia movement in north China whose martial-arts rituals they believed made them immune to bullets, killed missionaries and thousands of Chinese Christians in 1899-1900. In June 1900 Qing Empress Dowager Cixi backed them and declared war on the foreign powers. Boxers and Qing troops besieged the foreign legations in Beijing for 55 days. An eight-nation force, mostly Japanese and Russian, relieved the siege on 14 August 1900 and looted the capital; the court fled to Xi'an.",
 why="Resentment of missionaries, foreign concessions and the legal privileges of Chinese Christians came first. Drought and hardship across north China supplied recruits. The court saw a chance to turn popular anger against the powers that had taken leases in 1898.",
 then="The Boxer Protocol of September 1901 imposed an indemnity of 450 million taels of silver and stationed foreign troops in Beijing. Russian troops stayed in Manchuria, which set up the war of 1904. The Qing fell in 1911.",
 fact="'Boxers' was a Western nickname for the martial-arts rituals the rebels believed made them bulletproof."),
"jp-alliance": dict(
 bluf="Britain wanted a partner to check Russia in East Asia without adding to its own naval burden; Japan wanted assurance that no second European power would join Russia in a war. The treaty signed in London on 30 January 1902 bound each to neutrality if the other fought one power and to join in if a second entered. Japan could now fight Russia alone, and a Western power had taken an Asian state as an equal ally for the first time.",
 now="The alliance let Japan fight Russia in 1904 without fear of a second enemy and ran until 1923. It is the model for a global power partnering with a regional one to balance a shared rival.",
 story="British Foreign Secretary Lord Lansdowne and Japanese minister in London Hayashi Tadasu signed the treaty on 30 January 1902. It committed each side to stay neutral if the other went to war with one power, and to join in if a second power entered. It was Britain's first formal alliance in decades, ending its 'splendid isolation', and the first alliance on equal terms between a Western power and an Asian state.",
 why="Both feared Russian expansion in Manchuria and Korea. Britain also wanted to end its isolation and share the naval burden in the Pacific, and British yards had built most of Japan's battleships.",
 then="Two years later Japan attacked Russia, confident no European power would intervene. The alliance was renewed in 1905 and 1911 and ended at the Washington Conference of 1921-22.",
 fact="The Mikasa, Admiral Tōgō's flagship at Tsushima, was built at Barrow-in-Furness in England."),
"as190544": dict(
 bluf="Japan fought Russia to keep it out of Korea and push it back in Manchuria after talks on spheres of influence failed. It took Port Arthur, won at Mukden and destroyed Russia's Baltic Fleet at Tsushima in May 1905, at a cost that left its treasury near empty. An Asian power had beaten a European great power in a major war for the first time; Japan gained Korea and a foothold in Manchuria, Russia got the Revolution of 1905, and anti-colonial nationalists across Asia and Africa took note.",
 now="The victory made Japan the dominant power in Korea and southern Manchuria from 1905 to 1945, weakened Russian Tsar Nicholas II's regime enough to set off the Revolution of 1905, and showed nationalists from India to Egypt that a European empire could be beaten in the field. Its opening surprise attack prefigured Pearl Harbor.",
 story="In February 1904 Japan attacked the Russian fleet at Port Arthur before declaring war on Russian Tsar Nicholas II's government. After a siege that cost tens of thousands of casualties it took the port in January 1905 and won the land battle of Mukden in March. At Tsushima on 27-28 May 1905 Japanese Admiral Tōgō Heihachirō destroyed Russia's Baltic Fleet, which had sailed halfway around the world.",
 why="Rivalry over Korea and Manchuria: Russia's lease of Port Arthur and its railways threatened Japan's ambitions, and the Anglo-Japanese Alliance kept other powers out.",
 trig="The breakdown of talks over spheres of influence in Korea and Manchuria.",
 then="The Treaty of Portsmouth in September 1905 gave Japan the Port Arthur lease and southern Sakhalin. Japan made Korea a protectorate in 1905 and annexed it in 1910. Defeat sparked the Revolution of 1905 in Russia.",
 fact="Russia's Baltic Fleet sailed 18,000 miles around Africa to be sunk in one afternoon at Tsushima. On the way it fired on British fishing boats, taking them for Japanese torpedo boats."),
"jp-eulsa": dict(
 bluf="Japanese elder statesman Itō Hirobumi set out to take control of Korea's foreign relations by treaty rather than outright conquest. With troops around the palace in November 1905, five Korean ministers signed; Emperor Gojong withheld his approval. Korea became a protectorate under a Japanese Resident-General, the last step before annexation in 1910, and the signatories became the standing symbol of collaboration in Korean politics.",
 now="The protectorate stripped Korea of its diplomacy in 1905 and led to the loss of its sovereignty in 1910. The memory of ministers who signed under duress still shapes Korean politics, in which the charge of pro-Japanese collaboration remains a disqualification.",
 story="In November 1905, with Japanese troops surrounding the palace in Seoul, Japanese elder statesman Itō Hirobumi pressed Korea's cabinet to sign a treaty handing control of its foreign affairs to Japan. Korean Emperor Gojong refused to approve it; five ministers signed on 17 November, and Koreans call them the 'Eulsa traitors'. The United States and Britain had already accepted Japan's control of Korea. Itō became Resident-General.",
 why="Japan wanted Korea as a secure base on the mainland, and its victory over Russia removed the last power able to stop it.",
 then="Korean 'righteous armies' rose against Japan. Gojong sent envoys to the Hague peace conference of 1907 and was forced to abdicate in favour of his son Sunjong; Japan annexed Korea in 1910.",
 fact="Gojong's envoys to the Hague in 1907 were refused a hearing because the powers accepted that Korea's diplomacy belonged to Japan."),
"jp-portsmouth": dict(
 bluf="US President Theodore Roosevelt mediated to end a war both sides could no longer afford and to keep either from dominating East Asia. Russia conceded Japan's dominance in Korea, the Port Arthur lease, the southern Manchurian railway and the southern half of Sakhalin, and paid no indemnity. Japan became a recognised great power; its public, told to expect more, rioted, and the grievance fed later resentment of the United States.",
 now="The treaty confirmed Japan as a great power with Korea and southern Manchuria in its hands, and left Japan's public convinced it had been cheated of an indemnity, a grievance that fed militarism.",
 story="US President Theodore Roosevelt brought the two sides together at Portsmouth, New Hampshire, where Japanese Foreign Minister Komura Jutarō and Russian chief minister Sergei Witte negotiated. Under the treaty signed on 5 September 1905, Russia recognised Japan's dominance in Korea and handed over the Port Arthur lease, the southern Manchurian railway and the southern half of Sakhalin, and paid no indemnity. Roosevelt won the Nobel Peace Prize for his mediation.",
 why="Both sides were exhausted: Russia faced revolution at home, and Japan, though victorious, was close to bankruptcy and out of reserves.",
 then="Months later Japan made Korea a protectorate, while Tokyo crowds rioted over the lack of an indemnity.",
 fact="It was the first time a US president mediated a peace between great powers."),
"jp-portsmouth-b": dict(
 bluf="Tokyo crowds, told for months of great victories, gathered in Hibiya Park on 5 September 1905 to protest a peace with no indemnity. Three days of rioting killed 17 people, burned police boxes and churches and brought martial law to the capital. Popular nationalism had shown it could turn on the government that fed it, and later cabinets had to reckon with a street that punished any sign of weakness abroad.",
 now="The riots fixed a pattern from 1905 to 1945 in which Japanese governments feared being outflanked by nationalist opinion, and they planted the resentment of the United States as the power that short-changed Japan.",
 story="Japan's government had hidden how close the war had brought it to bankruptcy, and the press had promised an indemnity and territory. When the Portsmouth terms arrived, crowds gathered in Hibiya Park on 5 September 1905 and rioted until 7 September. They burned police stations and churches and attacked the home minister's residence and the pro-government newspaper Kokumin Shimbun. Seventeen people died, and the government declared martial law.",
 why="The government had hidden how close Japan was to bankruptcy, so the public expected an indemnity and new territory on the scale of 1895.",
 then="Prime Minister Katsura Tarō's cabinet fell in January 1906. Popular nationalism became a force Japanese governments had to reckon with.",
 fact="Hibiya was the first of a series of crowd protests in Tokyo over the following decade; one of them toppled Katsura's third cabinet in 1913."),
"jp-ito": dict(
 bluf="Korean independence activist An Jung-geun shot Itō Hirobumi, Japan's four-time prime minister and first Resident-General of Korea, at Harbin station on 26 October 1909 to punish the architect of Japan's takeover. Itō died within minutes; An was executed in March 1910. Japan annexed Korea ten months later, and the shooting became a patriot's act in Korean memory and a founding statesman's murder in Japan's, a divide that still separates the two countries' histories.",
 now="The killing removed a cautious voice in Tokyo and sped annexation in August 1910. An's status, hero in Seoul and criminal in Tokyo, is a standing point of friction; Japan protested when a memorial hall to him opened at Harbin in 2014.",
 story="Japanese statesman Itō Hirobumi, four times prime minister and chief drafter of the Meiji constitution, had served as Korea's first Resident-General until mid-1909. On 26 October 1909, as he arrived at Harbin station in Manchuria to meet Russian Finance Minister Vladimir Kokovtsov, Korean independence fighter An Jung-geun shot him dead. An was executed in March 1910. Itō had been among the more cautious Japanese leaders on outright annexation, though he had accepted it by then.",
 why="An Jung-geun sought revenge for Japan's takeover of Korea, of which Itō had been the chief architect. At his trial he listed fifteen charges against Itō, from the murder of Queen Min to the Eulsa treaty.",
 then="Japan annexed Korea in August 1910, ten months later, and ruled it until 1945.",
 fact="An had cut off the last joint of his ring finger early in 1909 as a blood pledge to kill Itō; his handprint with the missing joint is a national emblem in Korea."),
"as191046": dict(
 bluf="Japan set out to make Korea a colony and strategic buffer after beating Russia. The treaty of 22 August 1910, signed by Korean Prime Minister Ye Wanyong, annexed Korea and placed it under a governor-general backed by the army and police. Thirty-five years of rule developed Korea for Japan's benefit, confiscated land and in its last decade tried to erase the Korean language and names; forced labour and the 'comfort women' system of those years still poison relations between Tokyo and Seoul.",
 now="Colonial rule ran from 1910 to 1945, and its wartime mobilisation produced the forced-labour and 'comfort women' claims that Japan holds were settled by the treaty of 1965 and that Korean courts have ruled open since 2018. The dispute shapes relations between two US allies and the way history is taught in both.",
 story="Under a treaty signed on 22 August 1910 by Korean Prime Minister Ye Wanyong, Korea was annexed to Japan and Emperor Sunjong lost his throne. Japanese Governor-General Terauchi Masatake ruled with the army and police. Koreans' land was surveyed and much of it taken; later Korean was banned in schools and Koreans were pressed to adopt Japanese names.",
 why="Japan wanted to secure Korea as a strategic buffer and colony after defeating Russia.",
 then="Korean protests such as the March 1st Movement of 1919 were crushed. In the 1930s and 1940s Japan mobilised Koreans for its wars through forced labour, conscription and the sexual slavery of 'comfort women'. Korea was liberated in 1945, then divided by the United States and the Soviet Union.",
 fact="The treaty was signed on 22 August and announced on 29 August 1910, which Koreans mark as the Day of National Humiliation."),
"as191147": dict(
 bluf="Revolutionaries led by Sun Yat-sen and provincial elites angered by the court's railway nationalisation set out to end Manchu rule. A mutiny at Wuchang on 10 October 1911 spread province by province, and in February 1912 the six-year-old emperor Puyi abdicated, ending over 2,000 years of imperial rule. The republic had no army of its own and passed within a year to General Yuan Shikai; warlordism, Japanese invasion and civil war followed before the Communists won in 1949.",
 now="The fall left China without a central authority strong enough to hold it together from 1916 to 1928 and opened the contest between Nationalists and Communists over what should replace the empire. Both Beijing and Taipei honour Sun Yat-sen as the founder of modern China.",
 story="On 10 October 1911 an army mutiny in Wuchang spread as province after province declared independence from the Qing. Revolutionary leader Sun Yat-sen became provisional president on 1 January 1912. On 12 February 1912 the six-year-old Qing emperor Puyi abdicated, ending over 2,000 years of imperial rule.",
 why="Defeats by foreign powers and the Boxer indemnity came first, then failed reforms and the court's 1911 plan to nationalise provincial railways, which turned provincial elites against it. Sun Yat-sen's revolutionary alliance supplied the ideology and the network.",
 trig="An army mutiny in Wuchang on 10 October 1911, after a bomb exploded by accident at a revolutionaries' hideout.",
 then="General Yuan Shikai arranged the abdication and became president, then tried to make himself emperor in 1915. After his death in 1916 China split among warlords, and Nationalists and Communists competed to reunify it.",
 fact="The last emperor, Puyi, ended his life working as a gardener and archivist in Communist Beijing."),
"d-yuan": dict(
 bluf="Chinese President Yuan Shikai set out to secure his personal power by making himself emperor, convinced China needed a monarch to hold it together. He took the throne as the Hongxian Emperor in December 1915; provinces rebelled from Yunnan outward, he gave up the monarchy in March 1916 and died in June. The republic was left with no central authority, and China broke into warlord domains until 1928.",
 now="Yuan's death opened the warlord era, which lasted until the Nationalists reunified most of China in 1928 and which Japan used to extend its hold on Manchuria and Shandong.",
 story="Chinese President Yuan Shikai had pushed Sun Yat-sen aside in 1912 and dissolved parliament in 1914. He accepted the throne in December 1915 as the Hongxian Emperor, after petitions his own officials had arranged. General Cai E raised Yunnan against him and other provinces followed. Yuan abandoned the monarchy on 22 March 1916 and died on 6 June.",
 why="Yuan wanted to secure his personal power and believed China needed a monarch to hold it together. Flatterers and fake petitions told him the country agreed.",
 then="China broke apart among regional warlords, and Japan used the chaos to extend its influence.",
 fact="The Hongxian reign lasted 83 days."),
"as191949": dict(
 bluf="Beijing students set out to stop their government accepting the Versailles decision that gave Germany's rights in Shandong to Japan. About 3,000 marched on 4 May 1919; strikes and boycotts spread across the cities, and China's delegates withheld their signature from the treaty. The movement turned a generation of Western-educated reformers toward nationalism and, for some, toward Marxism, and its veterans founded the Chinese Communist Party in 1921.",
 now="The movement moved the reform generation from faith in Western liberalism to nationalism and, for leaders such as Chen Duxiu and Li Dazhao, to Marxism, which produced the Communist Party in 1921. Communists, Nationalists and liberals all claim its legacy.",
 story="On 4 May 1919 about 3,000 students marched in Beijing against the decision at Versailles to hand Germany's rights in Shandong province to Japan. Strikes and boycotts of Japanese goods spread to other cities, and China's delegation declined to sign the treaty. The movement became a wider call for science, democracy and a new culture.",
 why="The Versailles conference handed Germany's rights in Shandong to Japan rather than back to China, which had joined the Allies and sent 140,000 labourers to the Western Front. Japan's Twenty-One Demands of 1915 had already made it the target of Chinese nationalism.",
 then="The movement spread new ideas of science, democracy and writing in everyday Chinese. Veterans of it founded the Chinese Communist Party in 1921.",
 fact="China marks 4 May as Youth Day."),
"as192751": dict(
 bluf="Nationalist leader Chiang Kai-shek set out to destroy his Communist allies before they could take over the united front that was reconquering China from the warlords. On 12 April 1927 his forces and Shanghai gangsters killed thousands of Communists and unionists in the city. The Communists fled to the countryside, and a civil war began that paused for the Japanese invasion and ended in 1949 with Communists in Beijing and Nationalists in Taipei.",
 now="It is why there are two Chinese governments today, in Beijing and Taipei, and the unfinished civil war is the root of the Taiwan question.",
 story="In April 1927 Nationalist leader Chiang Kai-shek turned on his Communist allies and massacred thousands in Shanghai. The Communists retreated to the countryside, where Mao Zedong and Zhu De built a base in the Jinggang mountains, and civil war broke out between the two parties.",
 why="The Nationalists and Communists, allied since 1923 against the warlords, each meant to rule China. Chiang's backers among Shanghai's bankers and merchants feared the Communist-led unions.",
 then="The war paused in 1937 for an uneasy alliance against Japan, resumed in 1946 and ended with Communist victory in 1949.",
 fact="The Communist uprising at Nanchang on 1 August 1927, four months after the purge, is counted as the founding of the People's Liberation Army."),
"as193153": dict(
 bluf="Officers of Japan's Kwantung Army set out to seize Manchuria's land and resources without waiting for Tokyo's approval. They blew up a section of their own railway near Mukden on 18 September 1931, blamed Chinese troops and occupied the whole region within months; in 1932 they installed the last Qing emperor, Puyi, as head of the puppet state of Manchukuo. The League of Nations condemned the seizure and Japan left the League; the army had taken control of Japanese foreign policy, and the lesson that aggression paid travelled to Europe.",
 now="The seizure showed between 1931 and 1933 that the League could not stop a great power, and it moved control of Japan's foreign policy from the cabinet to the army, the first step toward the war with China in 1937 and the Pacific war of 1941.",
 story="On 18 September 1931 Kwantung Army staff officers Ishiwara Kanji and Itagaki Seishirō had a small bomb set off on a railway near Mukden and blamed Chinese troops. The Japanese army then occupied all of Manchuria and in 1932 set up the puppet state of Manchukuo with the last Qing emperor, Puyi, as its head.",
 why="Army officers wanted Manchuria's land, coal and iron and a buffer against the Soviet Union. The Depression had cut Japan's exports and discredited party government. The cabinet in Tokyo lacked the authority to stop its own army.",
 then="The League of Nations condemned the invasion, and Japan left the League in 1933. The army's success emboldened militarists at home.",
 fact="The explosion did so little damage that a train passed over the track minutes later."),
"d-longmarch": dict(
 bluf="The Chinese Communists broke out of their Jiangxi base in October 1934 to escape Nationalist leader Chiang Kai-shek's encirclement. About 86,000 set out; after a year's march across mountains and rivers perhaps 8,000 reached Shaanxi. The retreat cost nine in ten of the army, but it saved the party, made Mao Zedong its leader at Zunyi in January 1935 and put it in a northern base from which it grew during the war with Japan.",
 now="The retreat saved the Communist Party from destruction, made Mao its leader, and moved it to a northern base from which it fought Japan and won the civil war; its survivors ruled China until the 1990s.",
 story="In October 1934 about 86,000 Communists under leaders including Zhou Enlai and Mao Zedong broke out of their base in Jiangxi. Over a year they crossed mountains and rivers to Shaanxi in the north-west, where perhaps 8,000 arrived. At Zunyi in January 1935 Mao gained control of military strategy.",
 why="Chiang Kai-shek's fifth encirclement campaign, fought with blockhouses on German advice, had made the Communists' base in Jiangxi untenable. The party's Moscow-trained leaders had lost the argument over tactics.",
 then="From Yan'an the Communists rebuilt their forces, organised peasants and gained support as the most determined fighters against Japan.",
 fact="The crossing of the Luding Bridge over the Dadu River in May 1935 is the march's most celebrated episode, and the party's account of how hard it was fought is disputed."),
"as193754": dict(
 bluf="Japan's army sought resources and a sphere of control in China; Chinese Nationalist leader Chiang Kai-shek, pressed by nationalist opinion, could concede nothing more. A clash at the Marco Polo Bridge on 7 July 1937 became a full war within weeks. Eight years of fighting killed 15 to 20 million Chinese, drained the Nationalists while the Communists grew, and drew the United States into sanctions that led to Pearl Harbor; the Second World War in Asia began here, two years before Europe's.",
 now="The war exhausted the Nationalist state and gave the Communists eight years to grow from a remnant into a mass army, which decided the civil war of 1946-49. Its memory, above all Nanjing, shapes Chinese nationalism and relations with Japan.",
 story="On 7 July 1937 a night-time clash between Japanese and Chinese troops at the Marco Polo Bridge near Beijing escalated into all-out war. Japan took Beijing and Tianjin, fought a three-month battle for Shanghai, and in December captured the capital, Nanjing. Chinese Nationalist leader Chiang Kai-shek moved his government inland and fought on.",
 why="Japan's militarists sought resources and a sphere of control in China. Rising Chinese nationalism made further concessions politically impossible for Chiang Kai-shek.",
 trig="A night-time clash at the Marco Polo Bridge near Beijing on 7 July 1937.",
 then="China fought on, often alone, for eight years. Japan's war in China led to US sanctions and, in 1941, to Pearl Harbor; the Nationalists were drained while the Communists grew.",
 fact="Chiang Kai-shek's government moved its capital twice, to Wuhan and then to Chongqing, where it stayed until 1945 under years of Japanese bombing."),
"d-nanjing": dict(
 bluf="Japanese commanders, expecting China to collapse after the fall of Shanghai, let their troops loose on the captured capital to terrorise the Nationalist government into surrender. From 13 December 1937 soldiers killed surrendered Chinese troops and civilians and raped tens of thousands of women over six weeks; China's official count is 300,000 dead, the Tokyo tribunal of 1948 found over 200,000, and Japanese estimates run from tens of thousands upward. The government fought on from inland, and the massacre became the central wound in Chinese memory of Japan.",
 now="The massacre is the event through which China and Japan still argue over the war. Beijing holds the toll at 300,000 and treats denial as a national insult; Tokyo acknowledges the killing of many non-combatants and holds that the number cannot be fixed, while some Japanese nationalists deny the scale or the event.",
 story="Japanese troops under General Matsui Iwane and Prince Asaka Yasuhiko took Nanjing on 13 December 1937. Over the following weeks they killed surrendered soldiers and civilians, in mass executions on the Yangtze bank and in the streets, and committed tens of thousands of rapes. Estimates of the dead range from tens of thousands to 300,000: the post-war Tokyo tribunal of 1948 put it at over 200,000; China's official figure, set by the Nanjing war crimes tribunal of 1947 and carried by the Nanjing memorial, is 300,000; Japanese estimates run from the tens of thousands to around 200,000.",
 why="Japan expected China to collapse quickly after Shanghai, and commanders let exhausted, poorly supplied troops loose on the city to terrorise China into surrender.",
 then="China's government moved inland, first to Wuhan and then to Chongqing, and fought on. Matsui was hanged by the Tokyo tribunal in 1948; Prince Asaka was exempted from prosecution as a member of the imperial family.",
 fact="German businessman John Rabe, a Nazi party member, led the international Safety Zone that sheltered some 200,000 Chinese during the killing."),
"as194556": dict(
 bluf="US President Harry Truman's government used atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki to force Japan's surrender without an invasion of the home islands. The bombs of 6 and 9 August 1945 killed over 100,000 people at once and more by year's end; the Soviet Union declared war on 8 August and Japan surrendered on 15 August. The nuclear age had opened, with weapons able to destroy civilisation held in check since by the threat of retaliation.",
 now="Nuclear weapons have not been used in war since, and nine countries now have them; Russian nuclear threats during the Ukraine war and North Korea's growing arsenal test that taboo.",
 story="On 6 and 9 August 1945, on US President Harry Truman's authority, US bombers dropped atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki, killing over 100,000 people immediately and many more from injuries and radiation by the end of the year. The Soviet Union declared war on Japan on 8 August. Japan surrendered on 15 August.",
 why="The aim was to force Japan's surrender without an invasion that US planners expected to cost hundreds of thousands of American casualties. One reading holds the bombs decided the surrender; the other holds that the Soviet declaration of war, which ended Japan's hope of Moscow mediating, weighed as much or more, and that the bombs were also meant to impress the Soviet Union.",
 then="The Soviet Union tested its own bomb in 1949, and nuclear deterrence shaped the Cold War.",
 fact="Kokura was the primary target for the second bomb, but clouds covered it, so the plane turned to Nagasaki."),
"d-japansurrender": dict(
 bluf="Japanese Emperor Hirohito intervened to end a war his cabinet could not agree to stop, after the atomic bombs and the Soviet declaration of war. His broadcast of 15 August 1945 told the nation to 'endure the unendurable', and the formal surrender followed aboard USS Missouri on 2 September. A US occupation under General Douglas MacArthur remade Japan into a pacifist democracy and American ally, and keeping the emperor on the throne eased the whole process.",
 now="Japan remains a US ally under the security treaty of 1951, revised in 1960, and the pacifist constitution of 1947 that the occupation wrote is the subject of a revision debate that has run ever since.",
 story="After the atomic bombs and the Soviet declaration of war, Japanese Emperor Hirohito broke a deadlock in his cabinet. On 15 August 1945 his recorded broadcast told the nation to 'endure the unendurable'. The formal surrender was signed aboard the USS Missouri on 2 September before US General Douglas MacArthur.",
 why="Atomic bombs, Soviet entry into the war and the collapse of Japan's defences.",
 then="The United States occupied Japan until 1952, oversaw a new pacifist constitution in 1947, broke up the big business groups and carried out land reform.",
 fact="The broadcast was the first time most Japanese had heard the emperor's voice. Army officers had tried to seize the recording the night before and failed."),
"as194959": dict(
 bluf="Chinese Chairman Mao Zedong set out to found a Communist state after four years of civil war with Nationalist leader Chiang Kai-shek. He proclaimed the People's Republic from Tiananmen Gate on 1 October 1949 as Chiang's government fled to Taiwan. The world's most populous country came under one-party Communist rule; Mao's campaigns killed tens of millions, and after 1978 the same state produced the fastest sustained growth on record and a rival to the United States.",
 now="The PRC is the world's second-largest economy, the Communist Party still rules, and its claim to Taiwan and its rivalry with the United States define 21st-century geopolitics.",
 story="After defeating Nationalist leader Chiang Kai-shek's armies in the civil war, Chinese Chairman Mao Zedong stood on Tiananmen Gate in Beijing on 1 October 1949 and proclaimed the People's Republic of China. Chiang's government fled to Taiwan.",
 why="Nationalist corruption and economic chaos came first. Communist land reform won peasant support, the Nationalists were exhausted from fighting Japan, and Soviet help gave the Communists Manchuria.",
 then="Land reform killed many landlords. The Great Leap Forward of 1958-62 caused a famine that killed tens of millions, and the Cultural Revolution of 1966-76 brought chaos. After Mao's death, Deng Xiaoping's reforms opened the economy.",
 fact="The United States recognised the People's Republic in 1979, thirty years after its founding."),
"d-taiwan1949": dict(
 bluf="Nationalist leader Chiang Kai-shek moved his government to Taipei in December 1949 to hold the one province beyond the Communists' reach and to prepare a return to the mainland. One to two million soldiers and civilians followed, and martial law ran for 38 years. Two governments each claimed to be China; US protection after 1950 preserved the island, and Taiwan became a democracy whose status is the most dangerous dispute between Washington and Beijing.",
 now="Taiwan is a democracy of 23 million that Beijing claims as part of China and has said it may take by force; Taiwan's government holds that the Republic of China is already sovereign and that its future is for its people to decide. The dispute is the likeliest cause of war between the United States and China.",
 story="As the Communists won the civil war, Nationalist leader Chiang Kai-shek's government moved to Taipei in December 1949, followed by one to two million soldiers and civilians. Chiang imposed martial law, which lasted until 1987.",
 why="The Nationalists had lost the mainland civil war, and Taiwan, returned by Japan in 1945, was the one province beyond the Communists' reach across the strait.",
 then="The Korean War led the United States to protect Taiwan with its navy from June 1950. Taiwan grew rich, lifted martial law in 1987 and held its first direct presidential election in 1996.",
 fact="Taiwan's martial law, from 1949 to 1987, was one of the longest periods of martial law anywhere."),
"as195060": dict(
 bluf="North Korean leader Kim Il Sung invaded the South in June 1950 to unify Korea by force, with Soviet leader Joseph Stalin's and Chinese Chairman Mao Zedong's backing. Three years of war drew in the United States, China and the Soviet Union, killed about 3 million people and ended in an armistice near the line where it began. Korea's division hardened, the United States tripled its defence budget and committed to Taiwan and Japan, and China showed it could fight America to a standstill.",
 now="The war never officially ended, US troops remain in South Korea, and North Korea's nuclear weapons keep the peninsula a flashpoint.",
 story="North Korea, backed by Soviet leader Joseph Stalin, invaded the South on 25 June 1950. A US-led UN force under General Douglas MacArthur pushed it back almost to China; then Chinese armies entered and drove the UN force south again. The war ended in July 1953 near where it began.",
 why="Korea had been divided at the 38th parallel by the United States and the Soviet Union in 1945. North Korean leader Kim Il Sung wanted to unify it by force, and Stalin and Chinese Chairman Mao Zedong agreed to back him.",
 trig="North Korea's invasion of the South on 25 June 1950.",
 then="About 3 million people died. The United States tripled its defence budget and committed to defending Taiwan and Japan, while North Korea became a militarised family dictatorship.",
 fact="The 1953 armistice was a ceasefire rather than a peace treaty, so the war has never formally ended."),
"d-chosin": dict(
 bluf="Chinese Chairman Mao Zedong sent his 'People's Volunteers' into Korea in October 1950 to stop a US-allied Korea reaching the Yalu and to show the new state would fight the United States. They drove UN forces back from the border; at the Chosin Reservoir in late November US Marines fought their way out of encirclement in temperatures far below freezing, and Seoul fell again in January 1951. The war settled into stalemate, and Communist China stood as a military power that had met the United States in the field.",
 now="China dates its standing as a military power to this intervention, which it calls the War to Resist America and Aid Korea, and the stalemate it produced fixed the division of Korea from 1951 to the present.",
 story="As UN troops neared the Yalu River, Chinese 'volunteers' under commander Peng Dehuai attacked in late October 1950. At the Chosin Reservoir, from late November into December, US Marines under Major General Oliver P. Smith fought their way out of encirclement in temperatures far below freezing. By January 1951 Seoul had fallen again.",
 why="Mao would not accept a US-allied Korea on China's border, and he wanted to show that the new People's Republic would stand up to the United States.",
 then="The front stabilised near the 38th parallel by mid-1951, and two more years of fighting changed little.",
 fact="Marine survivors call themselves 'the Chosin Few'; temperatures at the reservoir fell to about minus 30 degrees Celsius."),
"d-inchon": dict(
 bluf="US General Douglas MacArthur landed at Inchon on 15 September 1950 to cut North Korean supply lines and free the UN forces trapped in the Pusan Perimeter. The landing worked within days: Seoul was retaken by the end of September and the North Korean army collapsed. MacArthur then pushed to the Chinese border, which brought China into the war; a victory had produced the overreach that undid it.",
 now="The landing turned a lost war into a drive to the Yalu within six weeks, and the drive brought China in. The sequence is the standard case of a victory producing overreach.",
 story="With UN forces trapped in the Pusan Perimeter, US General Douglas MacArthur landed troops at Inchon on 15 September 1950 despite dangerous tides. Seoul was retaken by late September, and North Korea's army collapsed.",
 why="MacArthur wanted to cut North Korean supply lines and relieve the trapped Pusan Perimeter with a landing far behind enemy lines.",
 then="UN forces pushed north toward the Chinese border, and China entered the war in late October 1950.",
 fact="Inchon's tidal range of about nine metres allowed landing craft over the mudflats for only a few hours on a few days each month."),
"d-koreaarmistice": dict(
 bluf="The UN Command, North Korea and China signed an armistice at Panmunjom on 27 July 1953 to stop a war none of them could win. It created a demilitarised zone along the front and produced no peace treaty; South Korea declined to sign. The peninsula stayed divided and formally at war, and the DMZ became the Cold War's most durable front line, with North Korea's nuclear weapons raising the stakes since its first test in 2006.",
 now="The DMZ is one of the most heavily fortified borders on Earth, and North Korea's nuclear weapons have raised the stakes of the unfinished war.",
 story="After two years of talks, stalled mostly over prisoners who refused to go home, US Lieutenant General William Harrison for the UN Command and North Korean General Nam Il signed the armistice on 27 July 1953. It created a demilitarised zone roughly along the front line.",
 why="Two years of stalemate came first, then Soviet leader Joseph Stalin's death in March 1953 and US pressure, including hints of nuclear escalation.",
 then="South Korea declined to sign, and the two Koreas remain formally at war. The United States signed a mutual defence treaty with South Korea in October 1953.",
 fact="The DMZ is about 4 kilometres wide and 250 kilometres long."),
"as195862": dict(
 bluf="Chinese Chairman Mao Zedong launched the Great Leap Forward in 1958 to overtake Britain's industrial output within 15 years by mass mobilisation into communes and backyard furnaces. Officials falsified harvests, the state requisitioned grain on the false figures and exported it while villages starved, and the famine of 1959-61 killed between 15 and 45 million people, the deadliest on record. Mao's standing fell, pragmatists restored private farming, and his bid to recover power became the Cultural Revolution.",
 now="The famine was hidden for decades and remains politically sensitive in China, where the party's official verdict speaks of 'mistakes' and protects Mao's standing; the leap is the standard case of a system in which fear of reporting failure turns error into catastrophe.",
 story="In 1958 Chinese Chairman Mao Zedong launched a campaign to overtake Britain's industrial output within 15 years. Peasants were herded into vast communes with communal kitchens and ordered to make steel in backyard furnaces, often melting down their own tools. Local officials, afraid of punishment, reported impossible harvests, and the state took grain on the basis of those false figures, even exporting it, while villages starved.",
 why="Mao's belief that mass mobilisation could leap past economic limits came first, then rivalry with the Soviet Union, and a political system in which no one dared report failure.",
 then="The famine of 1959-61 killed tens of millions; most estimates range from 15 to 45 million. Mao stepped back from day-to-day management, and pragmatists such as Chinese President Liu Shaoqi and party secretary-general Deng Xiaoping restored some private farming.",
 fact="Villagers melted pots and tools in backyard furnaces to meet steel quotas, producing mostly useless iron."),
"d-lushan": dict(
 bluf="Chinese Chairman Mao Zedong purged Defence Minister Peng Dehuai at Lushan in July 1959 for a letter criticising the Great Leap, to stop criticism of the leap from becoming criticism of himself. Peng lost his post to Lin Biao and the leadership closed ranks. Officials stopped reporting bad news, grain was still taken from starving villages, and the famine of 1959-61 went on to kill an estimated 15 to 45 million people.",
 now="Peng was cleared after Mao's death, in 1978; the purge is the standard case of one-man rule silencing warnings, and the Great Leap famine went on to kill tens of millions.",
 story="At a party meeting at the mountain resort of Lushan in July 1959, Chinese Defence Minister Peng Dehuai wrote Chairman Mao Zedong a letter about the Great Leap's failures. Mao denounced him as anti-party and replaced him with Lin Biao. Officials stopped reporting bad news, and grain was still taken from starving villages.",
 why="Mao treated criticism of the Great Leap as an attack on his authority, and no one in the leadership dared defend Peng.",
 then="Officials, terrified of being labelled 'rightists', kept inflating harvest figures, and the famine of 1959-61 killed an estimated 15 to 45 million people.",
 fact="Peng died in 1974 after years of persecution in the Cultural Revolution and was rehabilitated in 1978."),
"as196663": dict(
 bluf="Chinese Chairman Mao Zedong launched the Cultural Revolution in 1966 to purge 'capitalist roaders' from the party and recover the authority he had lost after the Great Leap. Millions of teenage Red Guards attacked teachers, officials, temples and one another; schools closed and millions of young people were sent to the countryside. Hundreds of thousands to millions died over ten years, education and cultural heritage were wrecked, and the generation that now rules China was formed by it.",
 now="Xi Jinping and many of China's current leaders were 'sent down' to the countryside during it, and the party discourages open discussion of the period.",
 story="In 1966 Chinese Chairman Mao Zedong launched the Great Proletarian Cultural Revolution to purge 'capitalist roaders' from the party. Millions of teenage Red Guards humiliated and beat teachers and officials, destroyed temples and relics, and fought one another. Schools closed and millions of young people were sent to the countryside.",
 why="Mao, sidelined after the Great Leap's failure, wanted to reassert control and revive revolutionary zeal.",
 then="Schools and universities closed for years and hundreds of thousands to millions died. Mao used the turmoil to purge rivals including Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping. The movement ended with Mao's death in September 1976 and the arrest of the Gang of Four.",
 fact="Mao swam the Yangtze at Wuhan in July 1966, aged 72, to show he had the strength for the fight ahead."),
"as197867": dict(
 bluf="Chinese leader Deng Xiaoping set out in December 1978 to make China rich by opening it to markets and foreign investment while keeping Communist Party rule. Farmers leased land, private business reappeared, and special economic zones from 1980 drew foreign factories. Hundreds of millions left poverty over 40 years, China became the world's factory and America's main rival, and the party kept its monopoly on power.",
 now="China became the 'world's factory'. Under Xi Jinping since 2012 the party has reasserted control over private firms, which puts the reform model's future in question.",
 story="At a party meeting in December 1978, Chinese leader Deng Xiaoping's reformers set China on a path of 'reform and opening'. Farmers were allowed to lease land and sell surplus crops, private businesses reappeared, and from 1980 special economic zones such as Shenzhen welcomed foreign investment.",
 why="Mao's campaigns had left China poor, and Deng judged that growth mattered more than ideology.",
 then="Hundreds of millions escaped poverty over the next 40 years. Growth brought inequality, corruption and pollution, and the party crushed demands for political reform in 1989.",
 fact="Deng's line: 'It doesn't matter if a cat is black or white, as long as it catches mice.'"),
"d-shenzhen": dict(
 bluf="Chinese leader Deng Xiaoping created a special economic zone at Shenzhen in August 1980 to test foreign investment and market rules in one corner of China before risking them nationwide. The zone drew Hong Kong capital across the border and grew from a town of a few tens of thousands into a city of over 17 million. The model spread to 14 coastal cities in 1984 and then across the country, and Shenzhen became China's technology centre.",
 now="Shenzhen is home to Huawei, Tencent and BYD, and is at the centre of China's drive for technological self-reliance in its rivalry with the United States.",
 story="In August 1980 the National People's Congress, at Chinese leader Deng Xiaoping's urging, created special economic zones at Shenzhen and three other southern cities, with low taxes and freedom for foreign firms. Shenzhen grew from a town of a few tens of thousands into a city of over 17 million.",
 why="Deng wanted foreign capital and technology without opening the whole country at once.",
 then="The model spread to 14 coastal cities in 1984 and then across China, drawing in foreign factories and making China the world's workshop.",
 fact="Builders of Shenzhen's International Trade Centre in the mid-1980s put up a floor every three days, and 'Shenzhen speed' entered the language."),
"as198970": dict(
 bluf="Students occupied Tiananmen Square in spring 1989 to demand political reform and an end to corruption; the Communist Party leadership under Deng Xiaoping decided to clear them by force. Troops entered Beijing on the night of 3-4 June and killed hundreds by the Chinese government's count of 1989, and more by foreign estimates. The party purged its reformers, chose economic growth without political liberalisation, and has followed that course since.",
 now="The party's choice of force over reform in 1989 set its course: economic opening without political liberalisation. The event is censored inside China; the party holds that the suppression secured the stability on which growth rested, and the protesters' supporters hold that it closed the path to political reform.",
 story="Reformist former party leader Hu Yaobang died on 15 April 1989, and students occupied Tiananmen Square calling for reform and an end to corruption; workers joined them and protests spread to other cities. Party General Secretary Zhao Ziyang urged conciliation and was removed; Premier Li Peng declared martial law on 20 May. On the night of 3-4 June the army cleared the square and surrounding streets. Chinese government statements of June 1989 put the dead at about 200 to 300, soldiers included; estimates by foreign governments and rights groups since then run from several hundred to several thousand.",
 why="Students demanded political reform and an end to corruption amid the highest inflation since 1949. The leadership was split between Zhao Ziyang's conciliation and Li Peng's hard line, and Deng Xiaoping sided with force.",
 then="The party purged reformers, tightened control of the press and universities and, after a pause, pushed economic growth harder.",
 fact="The identity of 'Tank Man', who stood in front of the column of tanks, has never been confirmed."),
"d-southerntour": dict(
 bluf="Chinese leader Deng Xiaoping, aged 87 and holding no formal post, toured Shenzhen, Zhuhai and Shanghai in January and February 1992 to restart market reforms stalled since Tiananmen. His remarks spread through the press and the party's conservatives gave way. The 14th Party Congress in October 1992 adopted the 'socialist market economy', and two decades of growth near 10 per cent a year followed under one-party rule.",
 now="It fixed China's path of market economics under one-party rule, which made it the world's second-largest economy.",
 story="In January and February 1992, 87-year-old Chinese leader Deng Xiaoping visited Shenzhen, Zhuhai and Shanghai, urging officials to be bolder and saying that markets served socialism as well as capitalism. His remarks spread through the press and pushed conservatives aside.",
 why="After Tiananmen, hardliners had slowed reform, and Deng feared stagnation would threaten the party's hold on power.",
 then="Growth surged, and in October 1992 the party adopted the goal of a 'socialist market economy'.",
 fact="Deng held no official post at the time; he had given up his last, chairman of the Central Military Commission, in 1989."),
"as199772": dict(
 bluf="Britain returned Hong Kong to China at midnight on 30 June 1997 because the lease on most of its territory expired, and China accepted 'one country, two systems' to keep the colony's economy intact. Hong Kong kept its own laws, currency and freedoms for a promised 50 years. Beijing's resistance to full democracy produced mass protests in 2003, 2014 and 2019, and the national security law of 2020 ended much of the autonomy as its critics read it, while Beijing holds that the framework stands.",
 now="The national security law of June 2020 ended much of Hong Kong's political autonomy as Britain, the United States and Hong Kong's democrats read the Joint Declaration of 1984; Beijing holds that the law restored order after months of violent protest and that 'one country, two systems' continues.",
 story="At midnight on 30 June 1997, in a ceremony attended by Chinese President Jiang Zemin, British Prime Minister Tony Blair and Prince Charles, Hong Kong became a Special Administrative Region of China under 'one country, two systems', with promises of a high degree of autonomy for 50 years. British Governor Chris Patten left with the prince aboard the royal yacht Britannia.",
 why="Most of Hong Kong's land, the New Territories, was held on a 99-year lease signed in 1898, which made 1997 the deadline. The Joint Declaration of 1984 set the terms of the transfer.",
 then="Hong Kong kept its own laws, currency and freedoms at first. Beijing resisted full democracy, which produced mass protests in 2003, 2014 and 2019, and imposed a national security law in June 2020.",
 fact="The handover ceremony took place in heavy rain, and Britannia's voyage home was the royal yacht's last before decommissioning."),
"as200173": dict(
 bluf="China's leaders sought WTO membership to lock in access to world markets; Western governments backed it in the hope that trade would liberalise China's politics. After 15 years of talks China joined in December 2001, cut tariffs and opened to foreign investment. Chinese exports grew about sevenfold in a decade, living standards rose, prices fell worldwide and factory towns in the West lost jobs; that 'China shock' feeds the tariff wars that began in 2018.",
 now="Trade tensions grew into US-China tariff wars from 2018. The political bet behind accession, that trade would liberalise China, produced a richer one-party state instead.",
 story="After 15 years of negotiation, led on the Chinese side by Premier Zhu Rongji, China joined the World Trade Organization on 11 December 2001. It cut tariffs and opened to foreign investment in return for access to world markets.",
 why="Deng Xiaoping's heirs saw global markets as the path to growth, and Western governments hoped trade would encourage political liberalisation in China.",
 then="Chinese exports grew roughly sevenfold in a decade. The term 'China shock' names the job losses in Western factory towns whose products competed with them.",
 fact="China's application began in 1986 under the WTO's predecessor, the GATT."),
"as201175c": dict(
 bluf="A magnitude 9.0 earthquake off Japan's north-east coast on 11 March 2011 sent a tsunami that killed about 18,000 people and flooded the Fukushima Daiichi plant, run by the Tokyo Electric Power Company. Backup power failed and three reactors melted down, the worst nuclear accident since Chernobyl. Japan shut its reactors for years, Germany decided to leave nuclear power, and the accident set the terms of the argument between nuclear safety and climate policy.",
 now="Debates over nuclear power's safety against its role in cutting emissions still turn on Fukushima; Germany closed its last reactors in April 2023 and Japan has restarted a minority of its fleet.",
 story="On 11 March 2011 a magnitude 9.0 earthquake triggered a tsunami that killed about 18,000 people and flooded the Fukushima Daiichi plant, operated by the Tokyo Electric Power Company. Three reactors melted down. Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan's government ordered more than 100,000 residents evacuated from the surrounding area.",
 why="The plant's sea wall and backup power were not designed for a tsunami that large; the wave of about 14 metres overtopped a wall built for under 6, and the backup generators sat in basements that flooded.",
 then="Germany decided to phase out nuclear power, and Japan shut down its reactors for years.",
 fact="Germany closed its last three reactors in April 2023, twelve years after deciding to leave nuclear power over Fukushima."),
"as201276": dict(
 bluf="Xi Jinping took the Communist Party leadership in November 2012 in a planned succession and set out to end the collective leadership and corruption he saw weakening the party. His anti-corruption campaign punished more than a million officials and removed rivals, and in 2018 the presidential term limit was scrapped. Xi became China's most powerful leader since Mao, tightened control at home and asserted China abroad, and the US-China rivalry took the shape it has today.",
 now="China grew more assertive abroad and tighter at home, and Xi began a third term in 2022-23 with no clear successor.",
 story="In November 2012 Xi Jinping became General Secretary of the Chinese Communist Party, and president in March 2013. His anti-corruption campaign punished more than a million officials and removed rivals including former Politburo member Bo Xilai and former security chief Zhou Yongkang. In 2018 the constitution's two-term limit on the presidency was scrapped, and in 2022 Xi took a third term as general secretary.",
 why="He was chosen by the party leadership in a planned succession from Hu Jintao. Xi and his allies judged that corruption and factional bargaining under Hu had weakened the party's grip.",
 then="In Xinjiang from 2017 the state detained large numbers of Uyghurs in what it calls vocational training centres against extremism and says closed in 2019; the UN human rights office said in 2022 that the detentions may amount to crimes against humanity, which Beijing rejects. Hong Kong received a national security law in 2020 after the protests of 2019. The presidential term limit was scrapped in 2018.",
 fact="Xi was sent to a village in Shaanxi for seven years at the age of 15 after his father, a revolutionary veteran, was purged under Mao."),
"d-wuhan": dict(
 bluf="Wuhan health authorities reported a cluster of unexplained pneumonia on 31 December 2019, and Chinese scientists identified a new coronavirus and published its genome on 11 January 2020. Wuhan, a city of 11 million, was locked down on 23 January, and the WHO declared a pandemic on 11 March. About 7 million deaths were reported by 2023 and excess deaths ran several times higher; early delays in disclosure and the unresolved origin of the virus became flashpoints between Washington and Beijing.",
 now="The virus's origin is unresolved. One reading holds that it passed from animals at Wuhan's Huanan market, citing the early cluster of cases there; the other holds that it escaped from a Wuhan laboratory, citing the city's coronavirus research and China's limits on an inquiry. The question keeps the pandemic a live issue between Washington and Beijing.",
 story="On 31 December 2019 Wuhan officials reported unexplained pneumonia cases; Wuhan doctor Li Wenliang had warned colleagues the day before and was reprimanded by police. Chinese scientists identified a new coronavirus within days and published its genome on 11 January 2020. On 23 January Wuhan, a city of 11 million, was locked down.",
 why="A new virus spread between people, including before symptoms showed, in a city with air links across the world. How it first reached humans is unresolved.",
 then="The WHO declared a pandemic on 11 March 2020. A WHO-convened mission to Wuhan in 2021 called a laboratory origin 'extremely unlikely', and the WHO's own director-general said the inquiry had been limited. About 7 million deaths were officially reported by 2023, and estimates of excess deaths are several times higher.",
 fact="Doctor Li Wenliang, reprimanded in January for warning colleagues, died of COVID-19 on 7 February 2020 and became a symbol of the cost of silence."),
}
